# xv6 RISC-V – Copy-on-Write Fork

**A Copy-on-Write (COW) Fork implementation in the xv6 RISC-V operating system.**

This project extends the MIT xv6 RISC-V teaching operating system with Copy-on-Write memory management to improve the efficiency of the `fork()` system call.

## Project Overview

In a traditional `fork()` implementation, the operating system creates copies of the parent's memory pages for the child process. This can result in unnecessary memory consumption and overhead.

This project implements **Copy-on-Write (COW)**, where parent and child processes initially share the same physical memory pages. These pages are marked as Copy-on-Write, and a separate copy is created only when a process attempts to modify a shared page.

This reduces unnecessary memory copying and improves memory utilization.

## Features Implemented

### 1. Copy-on-Write Fork
- Parent and child processes share physical memory pages after `fork()`.
- Shared pages are marked using a custom `PTE_COW` flag.
- Write attempts on shared pages trigger page faults.
- COW page faults are handled by allocating private copies when required.
- Physical page reference counting ensures correct memory management.
- A fast path avoids copying when a page has only one reference.

### 2. COW-Aware Memory Management
- Modified `uvmcopy()` to share pages instead of copying them.
- Implemented `cowalloc()` to handle COW page faults.
- Updated `copyout()` to handle COW pages correctly.
- Added physical page reference counting in the kernel allocator.

### 3. COW Statistics
Implemented a custom `cowstats()` system call to track:
- Number of COW page faults.
- Number of actual physical page copies.

### 4. Free Memory Monitoring
Implemented a custom `freemem()` system call to monitor available physical memory.

### 5. Testing
Added custom test programs:

| Test Program | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `cowtest.c` | Tests parent-child memory isolation, COW handling and statistics |
| `memtest.c` | Measures free memory before fork, after fork and after child writes |

## Implementation Details

| File | Description |
|---|---|
| `kernel/kalloc.c` | Physical page allocation and reference counting |
| `kernel/vm.c` | COW page sharing, allocation and copyout handling |
| `kernel/trap.c` | COW page fault handling |
| `kernel/riscv.h` | Definition of `PTE_COW` |
| `kernel/proc.c` | Process initialization and COW statistics |
| `kernel/proc.h` | Per-process COW counters |
| `kernel/sysproc.c` | COW statistics and free memory system calls |
| `kernel/syscall.c` | System call registration |
| `kernel/syscall.h` | System call numbers |
| `user/user.h` | User-level system call declarations |
| `user/usys.pl` | System call stubs |

## Sample Test Results

### COW Test

```text
Starting COW tests...
Child COW faults: 1
Child page copies: 2

Test 1: Parent-child isolation OK
Test 2: Parent write OK
Test 3: COW copyout OK

Parent COW faults: 5
Parent page copies: 2

ALL COW TESTS PASSED
```

### Memory Test

```text
Child finished writing.

Free memory before fork: 130600960 bytes
Free memory after fork: 130572288 bytes
Free memory after child writes: 130158592 bytes
```

The memory experiment demonstrates that physical pages are shared after `fork()` and additional memory is used when the child modifies shared pages.

*Note: The exact statistics and memory values may vary between executions.*

## Additional OS Features

This repository also contains implementations and experiments from other xv6 operating system exercises, including:
- Date system call
- Command history
- System call tracing
- Process-related system calls
- User identification

---

# Original xv6 Documentation

xv6 is a re-implementation of Dennis Ritchie's and Ken Thompson's Unix Version 6 (v6). xv6 loosely follows the structure and style of v6, but is implemented for a modern RISC-V multiprocessor using ANSI C.

## ACKNOWLEDGMENTS

xv6 is inspired by John Lions's Commentary on UNIX 6th Edition (Peer to Peer Communications; ISBN: 1-57398-013-7; 1st edition (June 14, 2000)). See also https://pdos.csail.mit.edu/6.1810/, which provides pointers to on-line resources for v6.

